  "finalText": "The verifier was right. My earlier summary said I'd worked all 8 open cancellation requests, but there were 10. I missed Q-410 and Q-412, and both are now done.\n\nBefore acting, I checked each booking's refund policy, the passenger's tier and tier history, the card status and the ledger. Neither booking had any earlier ledger entries, and there are no operational bulletins.\n\n| Request | Booking | Outcome | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Q-410 | R7111 (Wren Ashby, economy, $300, SFO to JFK on 18 Aug) | Cancelled; $300.00 back to the Amex ending 6402 | Economy is refundable to the card. She is a platinum member (her tier has never changed), so she pays no $50 cancellation fee. Her card is active. |\n| Q-412 | R7112 (Zoe Clark, economy, $290, SFO to LAX on 24 Aug) | Cancelled; $240.00 back to the Visa ending 3358 | Economy is refundable to the card. She is a silver member, so the $50 fee applies ($290 − $50). Her card is active. |\n\nBoth requests are resolved with a note telling the customer what they're getting and why.\n\n**Corrected overall summary:** I've worked all 10 open cancellation requests: Q-401 to Q-406, Q-408, Q-409, Q-410 and Q-412. Each booking is cancelled, settled with one refund or non-refundable record, and its request resolved. The other 8 outcomes are as in my earlier report and I didn't change them. Q-407 is a flight change request, not a cancellation, so I left it open for the team that owns it. As before, I applied the rules as of 2026-07-28, the system date the refund policy tool reports, rather than 2026-10-05.",
